  'matric-exams-start-smoothly-tight-timetable-concern-2026-10-15',
  'Matric exams start smoothly, but tight timetable worries support group',
  'Community', '2026-10-15', 'Rekord', 'https://www.citizen.co.za/rekord/news-headlines/local-school-news/2026/10/15/matriekeksamen-begin-vlot-maar-veeleisende-rooster-wek-kommer/',
  'South Africa''s 2026 matric exams began smoothly across all nine provinces on 13 October, though a school support group warns the timetable is unusually demanding for some subject combinations.',
  'The 2026 National Senior Certificate exams opened on Tuesday, 13 October, with candidates in Tshwane and across all nine provinces sitting their first papers. The Department of Basic Education said the day passed without serious irregularities, crediting months of preparation by national and provincial officials. The opening session included Computer Applications Technology Paper 1 Practical alongside several language papers. Basic Education Minister Siviwe Gwarube wished the class of 2026 well and urged learners to support one another through the exam period, which the department will continue to monitor.

Despite the calm start, the Solidariteit School Support Centre (SOS) has raised concern over how demanding the timetable is for some subject combinations, warning that certain learners face up to eight papers in eight school days. Pupils taking Mathematics, Physical Sciences, Accounting and English must write seven papers between 22 October and 2 November, including two sittings each of Mathematics, Accounting and Physical Sciences. On 28 October those learners sit English Paper 1 in the morning and Accounting Paper 2 in the afternoon, leaving little time to rest between sessions. Learners who add Engineering Graphics and Design and Geography to that load face eight papers over the same period. SOS school network builder Leandie Bräsler said the organisation asked the national examinations office in June to ease the schedule, without success.

Bräsler advised learners with approved exam accommodations, such as extra time, to confirm the arrangements with their schools now, since a day with two long papers can stretch considerably once extra time is added. She recommended early study planning, working through past papers under timed conditions, and splitting attention between questions according to their mark value. Getting enough sleep, eating regularly, staying hydrated and taking short breaks matter most on days carrying two papers, she said, and schools should route any problems through official channels rather than letting them go unreported.',
  NULL, NULL, NULL,
  '["https://www.citizen.co.za/rekord/news-headlines/local-school-news/2026/10/15/matriekeksamen-begin-vlot-maar-veeleisende-rooster-wek-kommer/", "https://www.sanews.gov.za/node/84871"]',
  'agent'
);
